---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-13",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-13",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "sole vs nordictrack",
      "nordictrack vs sole",
      "sole or nordictrack treadmill",
      "sole treadmill warranty",
      "treadmill without subscription",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Compare the Warranties", link: "#warranty" }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/compare/">Comparisons</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Comparison</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Sole vs NordicTrack</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        Sole builds the treadmill and lets you bring the entertainment; NordicTrack builds the treadmill around iFIT and charges for the classes that make it smart. Both make good machines. The right one depends on whether you want a platform, and on which part of the warranty you would rather have long.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated October 13, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS
        </div>
        <a href="#short-answer" class="btn-ghost">The Short Answer</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We research every product on this page independently. If you buy through our links, we may earn a
        commission at no cost to you. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Learn more about how we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          Sole and NordicTrack are the two names most often shortlisted together for a home
          treadmill in the mid-price bracket, and they are built on opposite ideas. Sole sells a
          heavy machine with long frame and motor cover, a plain or app-free console, and no
          subscription. NordicTrack sells a machine designed around iFIT, with a touchscreen on
          every model, automatic control during classes, and a membership that renews for as long
          as you want the content. Comparing them on screen size or motor rating misses the
          point. The real comparison is between two ways of owning a treadmill.
        </p>

        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">Where the figures come from.</strong> Every specification
          and warranty term on this page is taken from our <a href="/best-sole-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole range guide</a> and our <a href="/best-nordictrack-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack range guide</a>,
          both of which checked the manufacturer's current US listings against independent
          coverage on 13 October 2026 and carry only figures those sources agree on. Both brands
          keep model names while changing the hardware, and Sole's 2026 redesign means several of
          its model names now describe different machines from the ones most reviews cover. We
          have not tested these machines. Confirm the specification, the warranty and the model
          year for the exact unit before you order.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">Sole vs NordicTrack: The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#philosophy">Two Philosophies: Hardware-First vs Platform-First</a></li>
            <li><a href="#warranty">Warranty Structures Compared</a></li>
            <li><a href="#subscription">Subscriptions and Screens</a></li>
            <li><a href="#incline-decline">Incline, Decline and Speed</a></li>
            <li><a href="#deck-and-capacity">Decks, User Ratings and Build</a></li>
            <li><a href="#spec-confusion">Both Brands Reuse Model Names</a></li>
            <li><a href="#owning-one">Living With Each One</a></li>
            <li><a href="#who-buys-which">Who Should Buy Which</a></li>
            <li><a href="#what-to-buy">If You Are Shopping: What to Look For</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Sole vs NordicTrack: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy Sole if you want the longest frame and motor warranty, more incline on a
            folding machine, and no subscription at all. Buy NordicTrack if trainer-led classes and
            automatic speed and incline control are what will keep you training, and you will pay
            for iFIT.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            Sole's current listings give lifetime cover on the frame and motor for every folding
            model, incline to 15% on the F63, F65, F80 and <a href="/sole-f85-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>F85</a>, and an app Sole describes as free
            with no monthly subscription. NordicTrack's whole range runs iFIT on a built-in
            touchscreen, offers decline on the Commercial series and incline to 40% on its X Series
            trainers, and covers the motor for life only on the Commercial 1750, 2450 and X
            Series. Sole is weak on labour cover — 90 days — where NordicTrack gives a year.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are leaning towards Sole, our <a href="/best-sole-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ranking of the best Sole treadmills</a> sets out which 2026 figures are confirmed and which conflict. If you are leaning
            towards NordicTrack, the <a href="/best-nordictrack-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack range guide</a> does the same for its seven current models.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="philosophy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Two Philosophies: Hardware-First vs Platform-First</h2>
<p>
            The two brands make different bets about what a buyer is paying for, and nearly every
            difference below follows from that.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sole's bet is the machine.</strong> Its pitch has long been heavy frames,
            lifetime frame and motor warranties, and nothing standing between you and the
            treadmill. On the F50, F63 and F65 the 2026 consoles are plain white LED displays,
            with dial-style controls on the F50 and F63; you bring your own device for video. On
            the F80, F83 and F85 a 10.1-inch or 15.6-inch touchscreen adds streaming apps and
            Sole's free app. Nothing on Sole's listings suggests any speed, incline or programme
            feature is locked behind a membership, because there is none.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>NordicTrack's bet is the platform.</strong> Every one of its seven current
            treadmills has a built-in touchscreen running iFIT, which supplies trainer-led classes
            and outdoor-route workouts and can change speed and incline for you during them. That
            is a genuine training aid — it removes the decisions that make sessions easy to cut
            short — and it is an ongoing cost. Without a paid membership, a NordicTrack runs in
            manual mode with a few template workouts, and most of the screen's value goes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Neither approach is wrong. A buyer who trains best when someone else is setting the
            pace may get more use from a NordicTrack than from a sturdier machine they find
            boring. A buyer who wants to press start and run, and watch their own content on a
            tablet, will pay for features they never use on a NordicTrack. Settle which kind of buyer you are before comparing anything else.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Warranty Structures Compared</h2>
<p>
            Both brands split their warranty by component, and the splits are almost mirror
            images of each other. This table carries the terms as stated on our two range guides.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Component</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Sole (folding range)</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">NordicTrack</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Frame</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 years</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime on every folding model</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime on Commercial 1750, 2450 and X Series; 2 years on T Series and Commercial 1250</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Deck and electronics</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2 years (F63, F65); 3 years (F80, F85)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Parts typically 2 years</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Wear parts</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Parts typically 2 years</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Screen</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not listed separately; check the electronics term</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Labour</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">90 days</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Where Sole wins.</strong> The frame and the motor — the two most expensive
            components to replace — are covered for life on every folding Sole, from the cheapest
            60-inch F63 upwards. On NordicTrack you have to buy a Commercial 1750 or better to get
            lifetime motor cover, and the frame is ten years everywhere. If you want the
            manufacturer standing behind the drivetrain for as long as you own the machine, Sole
            gets you there at a lower price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Where NordicTrack wins.</strong> Labour. A year of labour cover against Sole's
            90 days means a technician's visit in months four to twelve is covered on a
            NordicTrack and usually at your expense on a Sole, even when the part itself is under
            warranty. NordicTrack's parts term, typically two years, is also longer than Sole's one
            year on wear items, though Sole gives two or three years on the deck and
            electronics.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The honest reading.</strong> Sole protects you against the big, rare failure;
            NordicTrack protects you better against the small, early one. Which you value more is a
            personal judgement. Terms vary
            by retailer and model year on both brands, so confirm them for your unit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="subscription" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Subscriptions and Screens</h2>
<p>
            This is the clearest practical difference, and the one most buyers underestimate.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sole: no subscription.</strong> Sole describes its app as free with no monthly
            subscription, and every model's console controls speed, incline and the built-in
            programmes on its own. On the touchscreen models, the screen runs streaming apps and
            Sole's app; it is a convenience, not a gateway. When you stop using an app, nothing on
            the treadmill changes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>NordicTrack: built around iFIT.</strong> NordicTrack says manual mode is always
            available without a subscription, and independent reviews describe the free tier as
            manual mode plus a few template workouts. The class library, automatic control and
            entertainment apps need the paid membership. Our guide to <a href="/can-you-use-nordictrack-without-ifit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">using a NordicTrack without iFIT</a> covers what stops and what keeps working in detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What that does to the real price.</strong> On a NordicTrack, the membership is
            a recurring cost for every year you want the classes, and over several years it can add
            up to more than the hardware did. On a Sole, the price you pay is the price. When you
            compare the two, add the iFIT membership for every year you expect to use it to the
            NordicTrack's sale price before deciding which is cheaper. Our <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT guide</a> works through that arithmetic without quoting prices that change.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What Sole does not give you.</strong> A class library that controls the
            machine. If automatic speed and incline changes during a class are the reason you want
            a screen, Sole's touchscreens do not provide them in the way iFIT does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="incline-decline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Incline, Decline and Speed</h2>
<p>
            On the confirmed figures, the two brands trade advantages here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incline on folding machines: Sole.</strong> The F63, F65, F80 and F85 all reach
            15% incline. NordicTrack's folding <a href="/nordictrack-t-series-vs-commercial/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>T Series and Commercial</a> series stop at 12%. For
            incline walkers, three extra percentage points on a folding treadmill is a meaningful
            difference.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Steep incline: NordicTrack.</strong> The X16 and X24 incline trainers reach 40%,
            a range nothing Sole sells comes close to. They do not fold and need serious headroom,
            but for hiking training they are in a class of their own.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Decline: NordicTrack.</strong> The Commercial series declines to -3% and the X
            Series to -6%. On Sole's current listings, the only model with decline is the TT8,
            which was sold out when we checked. Sole's 2026 F85 listing gives an incline range
            starting at zero, even though earlier F85s and many reviews describe decline — so if
            decline matters, NordicTrack is the reliable route, and our <a href="/best-treadmills-with-decline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">decline treadmill roundup</a> covers other options.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Speed: level, with one exception.</strong> Sole's folding models and most
            NordicTrack models top out at 12 mph. NordicTrack's Commercial 2450 reaches 14 mph, the
            only folding machine in either range that does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-and-capacity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Decks, User Ratings and Build</h2>
<p>
            Both brands sell 60-inch decks across most of their ranges, and the confirmed figures
            are close. The differences are at the edges.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Belt width.</strong> The Sole F63 has a 20 by 60-inch belt, and the F85 a 22 by
            60-inch belt that every source agrees on. Sole's 2026 listings give the F65 and F80 a
            20-inch deck where older sources say 22, so confirm the width for those. On
            NordicTrack, the T Series is 20 inches, the Commercial 2450 and X Series 22 inches, and
            the <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1250-vs-1750/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>1250 and 1750</a> widths are quoted inconsistently.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>User rating.</strong> Sole's folding models are rated to 350 lb at most on its
            current figures, with the F63 at 325 lb. NordicTrack's T Series is rated to 325 lb, the
            Commercial 1250's spec sheet lists 400 lb, and the X Series lists 400 lb. If the
            heaviest user in the house is near the top of those ranges, NordicTrack's higher-rated
            machines give more margin.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Build.</strong> Sole's reputation rests on heavy frames, and independent
            reviewers describe the F85 as stable and quiet. Sole lists the 2026 F63 at 187 lb and
            the F85 at 243 lb before packaging; NordicTrack lists in-box weights of about 250 lb
            for the T Series and 310 to 330 lb for the Commercial series. Those are not like-for-like
            figures, so we would not draw a conclusion about rigidity from them. Weight is one
            signal of stiffness, not proof of it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Step-up.</strong> NordicTrack's T Series has the lowest step-up of the two
            ranges at about 8.3 inches; an independent reviewer gives the Sole F85 about eight
            inches. For anyone with limited mobility, the T Series is the gentler option.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="spec-confusion" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Both Brands Reuse Model Names</h2>
<p>
            One problem the two brands share is that a model name is not a specification. Both
            keep names for years while changing what is underneath, and it catches buyers on both
            sides.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>On Sole,</strong> the 2026 redesign has made this unusually sharp. Sole's
            current listings give the F80 a 3.25 HP motor and a 20 by 60-inch deck, where most
            other sources still quote 3.5 HP and 22 by 60 inches. The F85's current listing gives
            3.5 HP, a 350 lb rating and no decline, where older sources give 4.0 HP, 375 lb and
            decline to -6%. Only the F63's headline figures have stayed consistent. Our Sole range
            guide sets the conflicts out model by model, and our <a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole brand overview</a> covers how the company builds and warrants its machines, though its model details
            predate the 2026 range.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>On NordicTrack,</strong> the motor ratings of the T Series 10 and of the
            Commercial 1250 and 1750, and the 1750's deck width and user rating, are quoted
            differently across sources and model years. Older names — the C-Series, the <a href="/nordictrack-x22i-incline-trainer-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>X22i</a> and
            X32i — have dropped off its site but still appear at retailers. Our <a href="/nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack brand overview</a> explains how the brand and iFIT fit together, but describes an older lineup, so use the
            range guide for current figures.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical rule is the same for both: read the specification on the manufacturer's
            current page for the exact model number, get the model year in writing if you buy from
            a retailer, and keep a copy with your order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="owning-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Living With Each One</h2>
<p>
            The ownership experience differs in ways that do not show on a specification sheet,
            and they are worth thinking through before you choose.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The first week.</strong> A Sole arrives, gets built and runs; there is no
            account to create and no trial to manage. A NordicTrack arrives with iFIT, usually
            with a trial or bundled membership period that renews automatically at full price.
            Note the renewal date on the day it arrives and decide before then whether you are
            using the classes enough to keep paying. On either brand, run the machine at the
            speeds and inclines you actually plan to use in the first week — listen for roller or
            motor noise and watch whether the belt drifts — because a fault found early is a
            straightforward return.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Year two and beyond.</strong> On a Sole, the frame and motor remain covered
            and nothing about the console changes; the costs that appear are labour after 90 days
            and wear items after a year. On a NordicTrack, the membership continues for as long as
            you want the content, the screen ages faster than the motor beneath it, and the
            software can change after you buy — both of which matter more on a machine whose
            screen is central to the experience.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The space and the power.</strong> Both brands' folding models are heavy and
            need planning: Sole lists in-use footprints of about 75 by 34 inches for the F63 and
            F80 and 78 by 38 inches for the F85, and NordicTrack's X Series does not fold at all.
            On both, manuals generally specify a dedicated grounded circuit, and a shared power
            strip causes faults that look like a failing machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep a record.</strong> Given how much both brands change their machines under
            the same names, note the model year and serial number from the frame label and keep
            the specification page you ordered from. If a warranty claim ever turns on which
            version you own, that record settles it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-buys-which" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy Which</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy a Sole if</strong> you do not want a subscription; you want lifetime frame
            and motor cover without paying for a flagship; you incline-walk and want 15% on a
            folding machine; or you would rather watch your own content on a tablet. The F63 is the
            Sole most buyers should start from: a 20 by 60-inch deck, 15% incline, a 325 lb rating
            and lifetime frame and motor cover, with every headline figure confirmed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy a NordicTrack if</strong> trainer-led classes and automatic control are
            what will keep you on the belt, and you will pay for iFIT; you want decline; you want
            steep incline from an X Series trainer; or you need 14 mph from the 2450. The
            Commercial 1750 is the NordicTrack most buyers should start from, because it pairs
            iFIT with decline and lifetime motor cover.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Either can work if</strong> you mostly walk at modest inclines and are
            undecided about classes. In that case, try the classes honestly: a NordicTrack trial
            month will tell you quickly whether you use them. If you would not, the Sole is the
            better long-term buy.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Consider ProForm if</strong> you want iFIT but not NordicTrack's prices. It is
            NordicTrack's sister brand on the same platform, with lighter machines and shorter
            motor cover; our <a href="/is-proform-a-good-brand/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">assessment of ProForm as a brand</a> sets out the trade-offs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>If You Are Shopping: What to Look For</h2>
<p>
            Whichever brand you lean towards, the same short list decides whether a particular
            machine is a good buy.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The motor warranty for the exact model.</strong> Lifetime on every folding
            Sole; lifetime only on NordicTrack's 1750, 2450 and X Series. That one line separates
            the good buys from the merely cheap ones on both brands.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The labour term.</strong> Ninety days on Sole, a year on NordicTrack. Budget
            for at least one paid service visit over the life of a Sole.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The incline and decline you will use.</strong> 15% on a folding Sole, 12% with
            decline on a Commercial NordicTrack, 40% on an X Series. Match it to how you train.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The five-year cost.</strong> Sale price plus any membership for every year you
            will use it. On a Sole that second line is zero unless you choose a paid third-party
            app.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The current specification.</strong> Particularly on Sole's F65, F80 and F85,
            confirm the deck, motor and user rating for the unit in the box.
          </p>
          <p>
            For the confirmed figures and the disputed ones on every current Sole, see our <a href="/best-sole-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole treadmill rankings</a>;
            for the NordicTrack side, our <a href="/best-nordictrack-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack roundup</a>.
            And if avoiding a subscription is the deciding factor, our <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a> roundup compares Sole with other brands built the same way.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Comparing Sole and NordicTrack</h2>
          <p>Most of these come from comparing the two on the same terms, when they are built on different ideas.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Comparing sticker prices only</h3>
              <p>A NordicTrack’s real cost includes the iFIT membership for every year you use the classes; a Sole’s price is the whole price. Add the membership to the NordicTrack’s sale price over the years you expect to own it before deciding which is cheaper.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Reading lifetime as covering everything</h3>
              <p>Sole’s lifetime cover applies to the frame and motor. Wear parts get a year and labour 90 days, so a service visit after the first three months is usually at your expense. NordicTrack’s lifetime motor cover similarly sits beside a year of labour.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming every NordicTrack has lifetime motor cover</h3>
              <p>Only the Commercial 1750, 2450 and X Series do. The T Series and Commercial 1250 have two years. If motor cover is your comparison point with Sole, compare the F63 with a 1750, not with a T Series.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Trusting an old Sole review</h3>
              <p>Sole’s 2026 F65, F80 and F85 differ from earlier versions on deck width, motor rating, user rating or decline. A review praising an F85’s decline may describe a machine Sole no longer lists. Read Sole’s current page for the exact model.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Sole is the hardware-first choice and NordicTrack the
            platform-first one.</strong> Sole gives lifetime frame and motor cover on every folding
            model, 15% incline and no subscription; NordicTrack gives iFIT classes with automatic
            control, decline and steep-incline trainers, plus a longer labour term.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you will not pay for classes, Sole is usually the better long-term buy; if the classes
            are what will keep you training, a NordicTrack Commercial 1750 earns its membership. To
            choose a model on the Sole side, start with our <a href="/best-sole-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">guide to the best Sole treadmills</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

