        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is Sole better than NordicTrack?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It depends on what you value. Sole gives lifetime frame and motor cover on every folding model, 15% incline and no subscription. NordicTrack gives iFIT classes with automatic control, decline on its Commercial series and 40% incline on its X Series, plus a year of labour cover against Sole’s 90 days. If you will not pay for classes, Sole is usually better value.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do Sole treadmills need a subscription?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. Sole describes its app as free with no monthly subscription, and every model’s console controls speed, incline and the built-in programmes on its own. The touchscreen models add streaming apps, but nothing on Sole’s listings suggests any treadmill feature is locked behind a membership. NordicTrack, by contrast, is built around a paid iFIT membership.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Which has the better warranty, Sole or NordicTrack?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Sole is better on the frame and motor: lifetime on both for every folding model, where NordicTrack gives ten years on the frame and lifetime motor cover only on the Commercial 1750, 2450 and X Series. NordicTrack is better on labour, with a year against Sole’s 90 days, and on wear parts. Confirm the terms for your exact unit and model year.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Which is better for incline walking, Sole or NordicTrack?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On folding treadmills, Sole: the F63, F65, F80 and F85 reach 15%, against 12% on NordicTrack’s T Series and Commercial series. For steep incline, NordicTrack’s X16 and X24 trainers reach 40%, but they do not fold and need plenty of headroom. NordicTrack also offers decline, which current in-stock Soles do not.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the Sole F80 better than the NordicTrack Commercial 1750?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              They suit different buyers. The F80 has a 10.1-inch touchscreen, 15% incline, a 350 lb rating, lifetime frame and motor cover and no subscription, but its motor and deck width are disputed between sources. The 1750 has a 16-inch pivoting screen, -3% to 12% incline, lifetime motor cover and iFIT. Choose on whether you want iFIT.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you use iFIT on a Sole treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Only in a limited way. The iFIT app can run on a phone or tablet beside another treadmill, but the automatic speed and incline control that defines iFIT needs a compatible iFIT machine, so on a Sole you follow the workout and adjust the console by hand. If automatic control during classes is what you want, buy an iFIT machine.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

